Live two-node wedge, appmana-023 route=0x1 <-> appmana-025 route=0x3, read
straight off the running fleet module (srcversion in each file, matching the
.ko on disk). Both ends were wedged and stayed wedged for hours, so this is a
stable state and not a snapshot of something in motion.

appmana-023, the sending end that is really stuck:
  data_tx_posted=53843 data_tx_completed=53811   (32 data frames outstanding)
  control_tx_posted=9488 control_tx_completed=8240  (and control keeps rising)
  tx_poll enabled=1 calls=31156 completed=107
  tx stall ... inflight=1269 posted=63320 completed=62051  (completed FROZEN)

Two things here at once, on one host, in the same dmesg:
  route=0x3  inflight=1 posted=11834 completed=11833   -- and five seconds
             later posted=11839 completed=11838. Both counters advance, so
             nothing is stranded. This is the detector artifact: only the
             supplemental poll used to stamp progress, and the interrupt path
             reaps nearly everything.
  route=0x1  inflight climbing past 1269, completed frozen at 62051. This one
             is a real stall, and note inflight exceeds the 1024 descriptor
             ring: the driver keeps handing control frames to a ring that
             stopped completing, and tb_ring_tx() returns 0 for every one of
             them because they land on the core's ring->queue with no
             descriptor. Data is fully gated off behind them
             (data_tx_enqueued=126307 vs posted=53843).

appmana-025, the other end, wedged with no ring involvement at all:
  data_tx_posted=29084 data_tx_completed=29084   (ring idle, nothing owed)
  data_tx_enqueued=95353                          (66269 packets never posted)
  tx_credits=768/768                              (window wide open)
  data_tx_errors=0                                (no post ever failed)

Every gate in tbv_path_schedule_tx() is open in that state except one: the
unframed raw-stream window. Held open by an owner with nothing left to send,
it refuses the queue head forever. See
tests/tx_ring_progress_test.c::tbv_tx_progress_cancel_closes_raw_window for
the cancel that orphans it.

Still open after this: why 023's ring stops completing at the hardware level.
The poll reads the descriptor at tail directly and does not see
RING_DESC_COMPLETED, so this is the NHI tx engine not draining for that hop,
not a lost or mis-handled descriptor. Settling it needs the ring's prod/cons
registers and the peer's rx ring occupancy at the moment it stops.
